## P1: The owner brake cannot merge the exact revert of a rules change

Observed on M. The rules@1 projection gate added after the original #350/#356 changes now breaks their post-merge brake for .warrant/v3.json. This is a current integration failure, not a claim that this rules@1 interaction existed at 8278d4a3 or a311e2b8.

At warrant/gate.py:720, _shared adds rules_reasons before _review recognizes the gate's exact revert. At warrant/gate.py:780-784, rules_reasons compares the reverted file with the newest rules@1 version still in the preserved store. open_revert (1266-1298) and revert_plan keep all signed records, so the rule version being undone remains in force. The revert is recognized as exact (facts.revert is its merge) but remains ineligible solely with RULES_PROJECTION. enforce_brakes then also regards it as pending (1445), leaving a blocked revert rather than restoring the prior rules.

The fixture first admits a valid constitutional change with green checks and the owner present, changing only an existing actor's replayer role in v3.json and filing its proper rules@1 successor. It merges with an open brake. The owner's hold on the original PR is recognized. The gate's own revert_plan is applied byte for byte to a single-parent bot-authored revert PR from the same repository; records remain intact, no path changed again, and its checks are green. Expected: that exact revert is eligible. Actual: exact_revert=true, eligible=false, only RULES_PROJECTION, zero rejected records and zero store problems.

Control: the same construction reverting AGENTS.md, with the same rules genesis, is eligible. Diagnostic control: temporarily disabling only rules_reasons in the in-memory interpreter makes the exact trust revert eligible too. This diagnostic isolates the cause; it is not a proposed fix or a change to this repository.

Impact: a timely owner veto cannot automatically undo a trust/profile change even when an exact nonconflicting revert exists. This differs from the named residual about a path changed again or records losing authenticity after restoring old trust: no added key, no authentication failure and no path conflict are needed here. The owner-brake contract from #356 and AGENTS.md rule 1 therefore does not hold for constitutional rules changes at M. Repair through an ordinary constitutional PR with a regression and a failing mutant, preserving signed history and an honest rules projection. No implementation was changed in this review.

